1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,499
This is ECE 320, video 14 b. It continues video 14 a with set-associativity and replacement policies.

2
00:00:08,949 --> 00:00:20,466
After this video, you can find the bits for every cache organization, and simulate a set-associative cache with LRU. Then we solve assignment 5 and the cache questions of two past midterms.

3
00:00:20,916 --> 00:00:33,036
Video 14 a used a direct-mapped cache with 8 frames. Each block can go in only one frame. So two blocks with the same index conflict: they need the same frame.

4
00:00:33,486 --> 00:00:41,149
Set-associativity fixes the conflict. The same 8 frames form groups called sets. The 2-way cache has 4 sets.

5
00:00:41,599 --> 00:00:50,980
Each frame in a set is a way. Each set has 2 ways, way 0 and way 1. A block can go in either way of its set. So the cache is 2-way set-associative.

6
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:56,956
More ways give fewer sets. A 4-way cache with the same 8 frames has only 2 sets.

7
00:00:57,406 --> 00:01:03,258
A cache with only 1 set is fully associative. A block can go in any of the 8 frames.

8
00:01:03,708 --> 00:01:16,664
The index selects the set. Count the bits of the set numbers. 8 sets need 3 index bits, 4 sets need 2, and 2 sets need 1. With 1 set, there are no index bits.

9
00:01:17,114 --> 00:01:19,715
The plus of more ways: fewer conflicts.

10
00:01:20,165 --> 00:01:26,388
The minus: a longer t hit. The cache must compare more tags, and a mux must select the data.

11
00:01:26,838 --> 00:01:36,855
Now make the 32 kilobyte cache of video 14 a 2-way. The blocks are still 32 bytes. Way 0 holds frames 0 to 511.

12
00:01:37,305 --> 00:01:41,020
Way 1 holds frames 512 to 1023.

13
00:01:41,470 --> 00:01:49,875
Each row is one set, with one frame from each way. So there are 512 sets: set 0 to set 511.

14
00:01:50,325 --> 00:02:00,728
512 sets times 2 ways is 1024 frames, as before. 1024 frames of 32 bytes hold 32 kilobytes.

15
00:02:01,178 --> 00:02:07,912
Now the address bits. A block has 32 bytes, which is 2 to the 5. So the offset is 5 bits.

16
00:02:08,362 --> 00:02:13,099
There are 512 sets, which is 2 to the 9. So the index is 9 bits.

17
00:02:13,549 --> 00:02:18,982
The tag is the rest: 32 minus 9 minus 5 is 18 bits.

18
00:02:19,432 --> 00:02:23,612
The direct-mapped cache had a 10 bit index and a 17 bit tag.

19
00:02:24,062 --> 00:02:34,650
Compare the two rows. Bit 14 moved from the index to the tag. Each doubling of the ways halves the sets, so one bit moves from the index to the tag.

20
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:45,085
The lookup. Take address hex C 0 4 4. Its 32 bits split into the tag, the index and the offset.

21
00:02:45,535 --> 00:02:51,525
The index is bits 13 to 5: 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 0. That is 2.

22
00:02:51,975 --> 00:02:57,687
Index 2 selects set 2. The cache reads both ways of set 2 at the same time.

23
00:02:58,137 --> 00:03:01,945
The tag is bits 31 to 14. They give tag 3.

24
00:03:02,395 --> 00:03:10,430
Each way has its own comparator. Way 0 holds tag 5. 3 is not equal to 5, so way 0 does not match.

25
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:18,356
Way 1 holds tag 3, and its valid bit is 1. A tag match and a valid bit give a hit in way 1.

26
00:03:18,806 --> 00:03:23,822
A mux selects the data of way 1. If no way matches, it is a miss.

27
00:03:24,272 --> 00:03:29,659
The offset bits, 0 0 1 0 0, then select byte 4 of the block.

28
00:03:30,109 --> 00:03:35,821
Slide 25 repeats the paper simulation with a 2-way cache. Pause the video and try it.

29
00:03:36,271 --> 00:03:43,330
The cache holds 8 bytes in 2 byte blocks: 4 frames. With 2 ways, the 4 frames form 2 sets.

30
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:46,752
A block has 2 bytes, so the offset is 1 bit.

31
00:03:47,202 --> 00:03:49,245
2 sets need 1 index bit.

32
00:03:49,695 --> 00:03:51,553
The tag is the other 2 bits.

33
00:03:52,003 --> 00:03:57,762
At the start, way 0 is the least recently used way in each set. The LRU column shows it.

34
00:03:58,212 --> 00:04:03,877
Access 1 1 0 0. Its index bit is 0, so it goes to set 0.

35
00:04:04,327 --> 00:04:15,194
Set 0 holds tags 0 0 and 0 1. No tag is 1 1, so it is a miss. The LRU way of set 0 is way 0. Replace way 0.

36
00:04:15,644 --> 00:04:20,985
Access 1 1 1 0. Its index bit is 1, so it goes to set 1.

37
00:04:21,435 --> 00:04:30,955
Set 1 holds tags 0 0 and 0 1. No tag is 1 1: miss. The LRU way of set 1 is way 0. Replace way 0.

38
00:04:31,405 --> 00:04:37,250
Access 1 0 0 0. Its index bit is 0, so it goes to set 0 again.

39
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:48,985
Set 0 holds tags 1 1 and 0 1. No tag is 1 0: miss. Way 0 was just used, so the LRU way is now way 1. Replace way 1.

40
00:04:49,435 --> 00:04:54,122
Access 0 0 1 1. Its index bit is 1, so it goes to set 1.

41
00:04:54,572 --> 00:05:04,789
Set 1 holds tags 1 1 and 0 1. No tag is 0 0: miss. The LRU way of set 1 is way 1. Replace way 1.

42
00:05:05,239 --> 00:05:10,255
Access 1 0 0 0 again. Its index bit is 0: set 0.

43
00:05:10,705 --> 00:05:19,246
Way 1 of set 0 holds tag 1 0. Hit. Now way 1 is the most recent, so way 0 is the LRU way.

44
00:05:19,696 --> 00:05:25,036
Access 0 0 0 0. Its index bit is 0: set 0.

45
00:05:25,486 --> 00:05:34,171
Set 0 holds tags 1 1 and 1 0. No tag is 0 0: miss. The LRU way is way 0. Replace way 0.

46
00:05:34,621 --> 00:05:39,311
Access 1 0 0 0. Its index bit is 0: set 0.

47
00:05:39,761 --> 00:05:43,337
Way 1 of set 0 still holds tag 1 0. Hit.

48
00:05:43,787 --> 00:05:53,539
The new DM column shows the direct-mapped cache of video 14 a. In that cache, the last access, 1 0 0 0, was a conflict miss.

49
00:05:53,989 --> 00:06:01,141
The plus: 0 0 0 0 and 1 0 0 0 are now both in set 0, one in each way.

50
00:06:01,591 --> 00:06:09,486
The minus: new conflicts can appear. 0 0 1 1 hit in the direct-mapped cache, but now it misses.

51
00:06:09,936 --> 00:06:12,862
In practice, conflict avoidance usually wins.

52
00:06:13,312 --> 00:06:19,860
Set-associativity adds a design choice. On a miss in a full set, the cache must choose one way to replace.

53
00:06:20,310 --> 00:06:28,158
An example, not from the slides. A 4-way set gets blocks A, B, C and D, at times t 1 to t 4.

54
00:06:28,608 --> 00:06:34,758
A loop then uses A again and again, at times t 5, t 6 and t 7.

55
00:06:35,208 --> 00:06:39,759
At time t 8, block E misses. The set is full, so one block must go.

56
00:06:40,209 --> 00:06:42,577
Random replacement picks any way.

57
00:06:43,027 --> 00:06:55,473
FIFO, first in first out, replaces the block that came in first: A, filled at t 1. But the loop still uses A. So FIFO does not match temporal locality.

58
00:06:55,923 --> 00:07:08,044
LRU, least recently used, replaces B. B has the oldest use, at t 2. LRU fits temporal locality: B is the least likely to be used soon.

59
00:07:08,494 --> 00:07:17,271
NMRU, not most recently used, replaces any block except the most recent one, A. It is easier to build than LRU.

60
00:07:17,721 --> 00:07:25,616
For a 2-way cache, NMRU is the same as LRU. With 2 ways, the way that is not the most recent is the least recent.

61
00:07:26,066 --> 00:07:37,212
Belady's policy replaces the block that will be used furthest in the future. Hardware cannot know the future. So it is an unachievable optimum. It is used only to compare the other policies.

62
00:07:37,662 --> 00:07:45,278
To find the least recently used block, each block in a set has an age count. In a 4-way set, the ages are 0, 1, 2 and 3.

63
00:07:45,728 --> 00:07:51,301
4 ages need 2 bits per block: 0 0, 0 1, 1 0 and 1 1.

64
00:07:51,751 --> 00:07:55,234
When a block is used or replaced, its age becomes 0.

65
00:07:55,684 --> 00:08:01,396
Every age lower than the previous age of that block gets plus 1. The other ages stay.

66
00:08:01,846 --> 00:08:07,836
An example. The 4 blocks of a set have ages 3, 0, 2 and 1.

67
00:08:08,286 --> 00:08:13,627
The program uses block 2. Its previous age was 2. Its age becomes 0.

68
00:08:14,077 --> 00:08:20,625
The ages lower than 2 get plus 1. Block 1 goes from 0 to 1, and block 3 goes from 1 to 2.

69
00:08:21,075 --> 00:08:25,905
Block 0 has age 3. 3 is not lower than 2, so it stays 3.

70
00:08:26,355 --> 00:08:34,528
Now the largest age is 3, in block 0. Block 0 is the least recently used. On the next miss, LRU replaces it.

71
00:08:34,978 --> 00:08:39,892
Slide 29 runs the algorithm on one 4-way set. Block 0 is empty.

72
00:08:40,342 --> 00:08:44,195
Blocks 1, 2 and 3 have ages 0, 2 and 1.

73
00:08:44,645 --> 00:08:47,942
A hit in block 3. Its age 1 becomes 0.

74
00:08:48,392 --> 00:08:56,845
Only block 1 had an age lower than 1. Its age 0 becomes 1. Block 2 has age 2, so it stays.

75
00:08:57,295 --> 00:09:02,124
A miss. The data moves into the empty block 0. Its age becomes 0.

76
00:09:02,574 --> 00:09:11,862
An empty block has no previous age. So every other valid age gets plus 1. Blocks 1, 2 and 3 become 2, 3 and 1.

77
00:09:12,312 --> 00:09:19,000
Another miss. Now the set is full. Block 2 has the largest age, 3. It is the least recently used block.

78
00:09:19,450 --> 00:09:23,072
The data replaces block 2. Its age becomes 0.

79
00:09:23,522 --> 00:09:30,270
Blocks 0, 1 and 3 had ages lower than 3. They get plus 1, and become 1, 3 and 2.

80
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:34,156
A hit in block 3. Its age 2 becomes 0.

81
00:09:34,606 --> 00:09:44,777
Blocks 0 and 2 had ages lower than 2. They get plus 1, and become 2 and 1. Block 1 has age 3, so it stays.

82
00:09:45,227 --> 00:09:51,032
Now block 1 has the largest age, 3. It is the least recently used block.

83
00:09:51,482 --> 00:09:55,568
Higher associativity gives a lower miss rate, because there are fewer conflicts.

84
00:09:56,018 --> 00:10:05,492
In the average access time of video 14 a, t avg equals t hit plus percent miss times t miss. The percent miss term goes down.

85
00:10:05,942 --> 00:10:09,982
But t hit goes up, because of the extra compares and the wider mux.

86
00:10:10,432 --> 00:10:13,997
The designer must balance both terms of the average access time.

87
00:10:14,447 --> 00:10:22,017
The block size and the number of sets are usually powers of two. Then the offset, the index and the tag are groups of address bits.

88
00:10:22,467 --> 00:10:27,529
The hardware takes the three fields out of the address with wires. It needs no arithmetic.

89
00:10:27,979 --> 00:10:38,056
Assignment 5, question 1. Find the tag, index and offset bits for three organizations. The addresses are 32 bits. Pause the video and try it.

90
00:10:38,506 --> 00:10:44,869
A block has 32 bytes, which is 2 to the 5. So the offset is 5 bits in all three cases.

91
00:10:45,319 --> 00:10:50,821
The cache holds 16384 blocks. That is 2 to the 14.

92
00:10:51,271 --> 00:11:04,878
Part a, 2-way. 16384 blocks in sets of 2 ways give 16384 divided by 2: 8192 sets. That is 2 to the 13.

93
00:11:05,328 --> 00:11:07,696
So the index is 13 bits.

94
00:11:08,146 --> 00:11:12,698
The tag is 32 minus 13 minus 5, which is 14 bits.

95
00:11:13,148 --> 00:11:21,460
Part b, fully associative. All 16384 blocks are in 1 set. So there are 0 index bits.

96
00:11:21,910 --> 00:11:25,347
The tag is 32 minus 5, which is 27 bits.

97
00:11:25,797 --> 00:11:36,896
Part c, direct-mapped. 1 way per set gives 16384 sets, 2 to the 14. So the index is 14 bits.

98
00:11:37,346 --> 00:11:42,036
The tag is 32 minus 14 minus 5, which is 13 bits.

99
00:11:42,486 --> 00:11:51,356
Compare parts a and c. From direct-mapped to 2-way, the number of sets halves. So bit 18 moves from the index to the tag.

100
00:11:51,806 --> 00:12:02,673
Assignment 5, question 2. Three caches, each with 8 blocks of 16 bytes and LRU. The addresses are 12 bits, in hex. Pause the video and try it.

101
00:12:03,123 --> 00:12:10,182
Take address 1 A 3. A block has 16 bytes, which is 2 to the 4. So the offset is 4 bits.

102
00:12:10,632 --> 00:12:16,437
4 bits are exactly one hex digit. So the offset is the last hex digit, 3.

103
00:12:16,887 --> 00:12:25,153
The first two hex digits, 1 A, are the block address: the tag and the index together. The tables hold these block addresses.

104
00:12:25,603 --> 00:12:32,337
Direct-mapped: 8 sets of 1 way. 8 is 2 to the 3, so the index is 3 bits.

105
00:12:32,787 --> 00:12:34,691
The tag is the other 5 bits.

106
00:12:35,141 --> 00:12:41,410
For 1 A 3, the index bits are 0 1 0. So 1 A 3 goes to set 2.

107
00:12:41,860 --> 00:12:48,919
Fully associative: 1 set of 8 ways. There are no index bits. The tag is all 8 bits of the block address.

108
00:12:49,369 --> 00:12:55,221
4-way: the 8 blocks form 2 sets of 4 ways. 2 sets need 1 index bit.

109
00:12:55,671 --> 00:12:57,742
The tag is the other 7 bits.

110
00:12:58,192 --> 00:13:09,802
The index bit is the last bit of the block address. 1 A is even, so the bit is 0, and 1 A 3 goes to set 0. Pause the video and fill the three tables yourself first.

111
00:13:10,252 --> 00:13:20,679
Part a, direct-mapped. Address 1 1 0. Its index bits are 0 0 1, so it goes to set 1.

112
00:13:21,129 --> 00:13:25,587
Set 1 is empty. Miss. Block 1 1 fills set 1.

113
00:13:26,037 --> 00:13:31,838
Address 1 3 6. Its index bits are 0 1 1, so it goes to set 3.

114
00:13:32,288 --> 00:13:37,396
Set 3 is empty. Miss. Block 1 3 fills set 3.

115
00:13:37,846 --> 00:13:43,558
Address 2 0 2. Its index bits are 0 0 0, so it goes to set 0.

116
00:13:44,008 --> 00:13:48,327
Set 0 is empty. Miss. Block 2 0 fills set 0.

117
00:13:48,777 --> 00:13:54,536
Address 1 A 3. Its index bits are 0 1 0, so it goes to set 2.

118
00:13:54,986 --> 00:13:59,212
Set 2 is empty. Miss. Block 1 A fills set 2.

119
00:13:59,662 --> 00:14:05,931
Address 1 0 2. Its index bits are 0 0 0, so it goes to set 0.

120
00:14:06,381 --> 00:14:11,722
Set 0 holds 2 0. Miss. 1 0 replaces 2 0.

121
00:14:12,172 --> 00:14:18,023
Address 3 6 1. Its index bits are 1 1 0, so it goes to set 6.

122
00:14:18,473 --> 00:14:22,978
Set 6 is empty. Miss. Block 3 6 fills set 6.

123
00:14:23,428 --> 00:14:29,512
Address 2 0 4. Its index bits are 0 0 0, so it goes to set 0.

124
00:14:29,962 --> 00:14:36,277
Set 0 holds 1 0. Miss. 2 0 replaces 1 0.

125
00:14:36,727 --> 00:14:43,275
Address 1 1 4. Its index bits are 0 0 1, so it goes to set 1.

126
00:14:43,725 --> 00:14:46,233
Set 1 holds 1 1. Hit.

127
00:14:46,683 --> 00:14:52,395
Address 1 A 4. Its index bits are 0 1 0, so it goes to set 2.

128
00:14:52,845 --> 00:14:54,982
Set 2 holds 1 A. Hit.

129
00:14:55,432 --> 00:15:01,551
Address 1 7 7. Its index bits are 1 1 1, so it goes to set 7.

130
00:15:02,001 --> 00:15:06,966
Set 7 is empty. Miss. Block 1 7 fills set 7.

131
00:15:07,416 --> 00:15:13,500
Address 3 0 1. Its index bits are 0 0 0, so it goes to set 0.

132
00:15:13,950 --> 00:15:19,291
Set 0 holds 2 0. Miss. 3 0 replaces 2 0.

133
00:15:19,741 --> 00:15:26,289
Address 2 0 6. Its index bits are 0 0 0, so it goes to set 0.

134
00:15:26,739 --> 00:15:32,079
Set 0 holds 3 0. Miss. 2 0 replaces 3 0.

135
00:15:32,529 --> 00:15:38,148
Address 1 3 5. Its index bits are 0 1 1, so it goes to set 3.

136
00:15:38,598 --> 00:15:40,502
Set 3 holds 1 3. Hit.

137
00:15:40,952 --> 00:15:45,225
Count the No answers in the Hit column: 10 misses in 13 accesses.

138
00:15:45,675 --> 00:15:51,666
Blocks 2 0, 1 0 and 3 0 all need set 0. So they kick each other out.

139
00:15:52,116 --> 00:16:02,790
Part b, fully associative. There is 1 set of 8 ways, W 0 to W 7. A block can go in any way, and the tag is the whole block address.

140
00:16:03,240 --> 00:16:16,429
The first six accesses are six different blocks: 1 1, 1 3, 2 0, 1 A, 1 0 and 3 6. Each one misses and fills the next empty way, W 0 to W 5.

141
00:16:16,879 --> 00:16:21,895
2 0 4. Block 2 0 is still in W 2. Hit.

142
00:16:22,345 --> 00:16:26,710
1 1 4. Block 1 1 is in W 0. Hit.

143
00:16:27,160 --> 00:16:31,993
1 A 4. Block 1 A is in W 3. Hit.

144
00:16:32,443 --> 00:16:38,759
1 7 7. Block 1 7 is new. Miss. It fills the empty W 6.

145
00:16:39,209 --> 00:16:46,779
3 0 1. Block 3 0 is new. Miss. It fills W 7. Now all 8 ways are full.

146
00:16:47,229 --> 00:16:52,430
2 0 6. Block 2 0 is in W 2. Hit.

147
00:16:52,880 --> 00:16:57,988
1 3 5. Block 1 3 is in W 1. Hit.

148
00:16:58,438 --> 00:17:02,426
Count the No answers: 8 misses in 13 accesses.

149
00:17:02,876 --> 00:17:10,121
There are exactly 8 different blocks, one in each way. Each block misses only once, the first time.

150
00:17:10,571 --> 00:17:22,506
Part c, 4-way. There are 2 sets of 4 ways. The index is the last bit of the block address. Even block addresses go to set 0, and odd ones go to set 1.

151
00:17:22,956 --> 00:17:31,809
1 1 0 and 1 3 6 have odd block addresses, 1 1 and 1 3. They go to set 1 and fill ways 0 and 1.

152
00:17:32,259 --> 00:17:43,079
2 0 2, 1 A 3, 1 0 2 and 3 6 1 have even block addresses. They fill the 4 ways of set 0. Now set 0 is full.

153
00:17:43,529 --> 00:17:49,056
2 0 4. Block 2 0 is in set 0, way 0. Hit.

154
00:17:49,506 --> 00:17:53,453
1 1 4. Block 1 1 is in set 1, way 0. Hit.

155
00:17:53,903 --> 00:17:59,526
1 A 4. Block 1 A is in set 0, way 1. Hit.

156
00:17:59,976 --> 00:18:07,035
1 7 7. 1 7 is odd, so set 1. Miss. It fills the empty way 2 of set 1.

157
00:18:07,485 --> 00:18:16,912
3 0 1. 3 0 is even, so set 0. Miss, and set 0 is full. LRU must choose a block to replace.

158
00:18:17,362 --> 00:18:20,427
List the uses of set 0, oldest first.

159
00:18:20,877 --> 00:18:25,707
2 0 and 1 A were used again later. So their first uses do not count.

160
00:18:26,157 --> 00:18:34,144
Of the other uses, 1 0 is the oldest, because 3 6 came after it. So 1 0 is the least recently used block.

161
00:18:34,594 --> 00:18:38,402
3 0 replaces 1 0 in way 2 of set 0.

162
00:18:38,852 --> 00:18:44,425
2 0 6. Block 2 0 is in set 0, way 0. Hit.

163
00:18:44,875 --> 00:18:49,241
1 3 5. Block 1 3 is in set 1, way 1. Hit.

164
00:18:49,691 --> 00:18:55,263
The summary. Each square is one access. Direct-mapped: 10 misses in 13.

165
00:18:55,713 --> 00:18:58,918
Fully associative: 8 misses in 13.

166
00:18:59,368 --> 00:19:02,293
4-way: also 8 misses in 13.

167
00:19:02,743 --> 00:19:13,471
10 minus 8 is 2. These 2 misses, at 2 0 4 and 2 0 6, were conflict misses in the direct-mapped cache. Associativity removed them.

168
00:19:13,921 --> 00:19:27,064
Now the Fall 2022 midterm, question 2. Part A, 3 marks. Two 4-way caches with 16 bit addresses. Find the tag, index and offset bits of both. Pause the video and try it.

169
00:19:27,514 --> 00:19:34,851
Cache 1. 1024 bytes in 16 byte blocks give 1024 divided by 16: 64 blocks.

170
00:19:35,301 --> 00:19:44,078
64 blocks in sets of 4 ways give 64 divided by 4: 16 sets. The grid shows them: 16 rows of 4 ways.

171
00:19:44,528 --> 00:19:49,311
A block has 16 bytes, which is 2 to the 4. So the offset is 4 bits.

172
00:19:49,761 --> 00:19:53,662
16 sets is 2 to the 4, so the index is 4 bits.

173
00:19:54,112 --> 00:19:58,524
The tag is 16 minus 4 minus 4, which is 8 bits.

174
00:19:58,974 --> 00:20:09,886
Cache 2. 16384 bytes in 256 byte blocks also give 64 blocks. So it also has 16 sets of 4 ways.

175
00:20:10,336 --> 00:20:16,048
A block has 256 bytes, which is 2 to the 8. So the offset is 8 bits.

176
00:20:16,498 --> 00:20:19,423
16 sets again give a 4 bit index.

177
00:20:19,873 --> 00:20:23,858
The tag is 16 minus 4 minus 8, which is 4 bits.

178
00:20:24,308 --> 00:20:30,484
Both caches have 16 sets. The larger blocks move 4 bits from the tag to the offset.

179
00:20:30,934 --> 00:20:41,383
Part B, 5 marks. Find which cache gives the higher hit rate for the program in the question. First, find the addresses. Pause the video and try it.

180
00:20:41,833 --> 00:20:50,471
Each row of A has 4096 bytes. That is hex 1 0 0 0. So row j of A starts at hex j 0 0 0.

181
00:20:50,921 --> 00:20:58,259
Each element is 4 bytes. So A of j, i is at 4096 times j plus 4 times i.

182
00:20:58,709 --> 00:21:07,532
B starts at hex 4 0 1 0, after the rows of A. So B of i is at hex 4 0 1 0 plus 4 times i.

183
00:21:07,982 --> 00:21:19,777
The inner loop runs over j. So the program walks down column 0 of A. It writes A of 0, 0, then A of 1, 0, A of 2, 0 and A of 3, 0. Then it writes B of 0.

184
00:21:20,227 --> 00:21:23,849
Then the same for i equal to 1. That gives 10 addresses.

185
00:21:24,299 --> 00:21:33,448
Cache 1 has 16 byte blocks. Its tag is bits 15 to 8, the index is bits 7 to 4, and the offset is bits 3 to 0.

186
00:21:33,898 --> 00:21:48,149
The first four addresses are hex 0, 1 0 0 0, 2 0 0 0 and 3 0 0 0. Their index bits, 7 to 4, are all 0. So all four go to set 0.

187
00:21:48,599 --> 00:21:56,308
Their tags are 0 0, 1 0, 2 0 and 3 0. Four misses fill the 4 ways of set 0.

188
00:21:56,758 --> 00:22:07,346
B of 0 is at hex 4 0 1 0. Its index bits, 7 to 4, are 0 0 0 1. So it goes to set 1.

189
00:22:07,796 --> 00:22:12,115
Its tag is 4 0. Set 1 is empty, so it is a miss.

190
00:22:12,565 --> 00:22:22,317
Now i is 1. A of 0, 1 is at hex 4. Only its offset differs from hex 0. So it is in the same 16 byte block as A of 0, 0. Hit.

191
00:22:22,767 --> 00:22:27,825
A of 1, 1, A of 2, 1 and A of 3, 1 hit for the same reason.

192
00:22:28,275 --> 00:22:33,941
B of 1 is at hex 4 0 1 4. Only its offset differs from B of 0. Hit.

193
00:22:34,391 --> 00:22:40,521
Cache 1 has 5 hits in 10 accesses. The hit rate is 5 divided by 10: 50 percent.

194
00:22:40,971 --> 00:22:50,538
Cache 2 has 256 byte blocks. Its tag is bits 15 to 12, the index is bits 11 to 8, and the offset is bits 7 to 0.

195
00:22:50,988 --> 00:23:03,016
Look at hex 0, 1 0 0 0, 2 0 0 0 and 3 0 0 0. Their index bits, 11 to 8, are all 0. So all four go to set 0.

196
00:23:03,466 --> 00:23:09,921
Their tags are 0, 1, 2 and 3. Four misses fill the 4 ways of set 0.

197
00:23:10,371 --> 00:23:20,309
B of 0 is at hex 4 0 1 0. Its index bits, 11 to 8, are also 0 0 0 0. So it also goes to set 0.

198
00:23:20,759 --> 00:23:28,607
Its tag is 4. Set 0 is full. The least recently used block is tag 0, so tag 4 replaces it.

199
00:23:29,057 --> 00:23:35,141
Now A of 0, 1 at hex 4. Its tag is 0, which was just evicted. Miss.

200
00:23:35,591 --> 00:23:40,281
LRU now evicts tag 1. Tag 1 is the next block that the program needs.

201
00:23:40,731 --> 00:23:49,648
The same pattern repeats. Five blocks take turns in a 4-way set. LRU always evicts the block that comes next, so every access misses.

202
00:23:50,098 --> 00:23:52,977
Cache 2 has 0 hits in 10 accesses.

203
00:23:53,427 --> 00:23:59,232
So cache 1, with the 16 byte blocks, has the higher hit rate: 50 percent against 0 percent.

204
00:23:59,682 --> 00:24:08,088
Part C, 2 marks. Find a change that gives cache 2 a higher hit rate, with the same cache size. Pause the video and try it.

205
00:24:08,538 --> 00:24:13,832
The problem: 5 blocks map to set 0, but the set has only 4 ways.

206
00:24:14,282 --> 00:24:24,267
Keep 16384 bytes and 256 byte blocks, but make the cache 8-way. 64 blocks in sets of 8 ways give 8 sets. So the index is 3 bits.

207
00:24:24,717 --> 00:24:28,664
The tag is 16 minus 3 minus 8, which is 5 bits.

208
00:24:29,114 --> 00:24:38,355
The 5 blocks still go to set 0, now with the 5 bit tags 0, 2, 4, 6 and 8. All 5 fit in the 8 ways.

209
00:24:38,805 --> 00:24:46,793
So there are 5 misses for i equal to 0, then 5 hits for i equal to 1. 5 hits in 10 is 50 percent.

210
00:24:47,243 --> 00:24:55,231
Smaller blocks also work. With 16 byte blocks, A goes to set 0 and B to set 1: also 50 percent.

211
00:24:55,681 --> 00:25:02,368
So the answer is yes. The misses are conflict misses: raise the associativity, or use smaller blocks.

212
00:25:02,818 --> 00:25:18,701
The last exam question: Fall 2019, question 3. A 32 kilobyte, 4-way cache with 16 byte blocks. Part A asks for the bits. Part B asks for 5 addresses in the same set but in different ways. Pause the video and try it.

213
00:25:19,151 --> 00:25:24,770
Part A. A block has 16 bytes, which is 2 to the 4. So the offset is 4 bits.

214
00:25:25,220 --> 00:25:29,492
32 kilobytes divided by 16 bytes is 2048 blocks.

215
00:25:29,942 --> 00:25:38,627
2048 blocks divided by 4 ways is 512 sets. 512 is 2 to the 9, so the index is 9 bits.

216
00:25:39,077 --> 00:25:49,892
The paper does not give the address width. Assume 32 bits, as in RISC-V, and say so. Then the tag is 32 minus 9 minus 4, which is 19 bits.

217
00:25:50,342 --> 00:25:55,171
Part B. Same set means the same index bits. Use set 0.

218
00:25:55,621 --> 00:26:01,658
Different ways means different tags. So keep the index and the offset at 0, and change only the tag.

219
00:26:02,108 --> 00:26:12,650
The tag starts at bit 13. So tag plus 1 adds 2 to the 13 to the address. That is 512 sets times 16 bytes: hex 2 0 0 0.

220
00:26:13,100 --> 00:26:24,571
So A to E are hex 0, 2 0 0 0, 4 0 0 0, 6 0 0 0 and 8 0 0 0. Their tags are 0 to 4.

221
00:26:25,021 --> 00:26:40,393
Part C. The table shows 15 accesses to one set. They go to ways 0, 1, 2, 3, 0, 1, and so on, and every access misses. Fill in the labels A to E. Pause the video and try it.

222
00:26:40,843 --> 00:26:45,254
The first four accesses fill the empty ways: A, B, C and D.

223
00:26:45,704 --> 00:26:54,760
Access 5 must miss and go to way 0. So it is a new block, E. LRU replaces A, the least recently used block, in way 0.

224
00:26:55,210 --> 00:27:03,941
Access 6 must miss and go to way 1. A was just evicted, so access A again. It misses, and LRU replaces B in way 1.

225
00:27:04,391 --> 00:27:17,248
The pattern continues: B, C, D, E, A, B, C, D, E. Each access asks for the block that LRU evicted last.

226
00:27:17,698 --> 00:27:25,222
The rule: 5 blocks that cycle through a 4-way LRU set miss every time, as in cache 2 of Fall 2022.

227
00:27:25,672 --> 00:27:35,052
The recap. One. Sets equal blocks divided by ways. Direct-mapped has 1 way. Fully associative has 1 set.

228
00:27:35,502 --> 00:27:43,444
Two. The offset is log 2 of the block size. The index is log 2 of the number of sets. The tag is the rest.

229
00:27:43,894 --> 00:27:48,909
Three. The index selects the set, and all tags of the set are compared in parallel.

230
00:27:49,359 --> 00:27:54,096
Four. Associativity removes conflicts but makes t hit longer.

231
00:27:54,546 --> 00:28:03,834
Five. LRU ages: the used block gets age 0, and the lower ages get plus 1. The largest age is the victim.

232
00:28:04,284 --> 00:28:08,882
Six. With 16 byte blocks, the last hex digit is the offset.

233
00:28:09,332 --> 00:28:22,381
Seven. N plus 1 blocks in an N-way LRU set miss every time. Video 15 covers misses, writes and the performance of the hierarchy. That is the end of video 14 b.
